#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166788 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166626 :question/id #uuid "aeb0ab38-6a30-586d-bcb4-8d3c6b8c9d9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8409" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} + y + 6 = y^2.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-3$\n\n- $y=-2$\n\n- $y=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(t) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} = y^2 - y - 6,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y) = y^2 - y - 6.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-3$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-3) = (-3)^2 - (-3) - 6 = 6 \\neq 0.$  $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\n- $y = -2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-2) = (-2)^2 - (-2) - 6 = 0.$$\\quad  \\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\n- $y= -1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1) = (-1)^2 - (-1) - 6 = -4 \\neq 0.$ $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a6ca425-0e94-5b83-8729-fda13fbf3d7d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048369 :topic/id #uuid "99e1fe28-92a1-545f-8ee9-b78506123551" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068750}] :topic/math-academy-id 469 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050364}] :topic/title "Determining the Roots of Polynomials"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186748370 :question-generator/id #uuid "ac3f4ddf-e1ea-578a-85d0-b59d3a1b9f79" :question-generator/script "generators/4f/4fd06a5b0cf48eeed6d10418e1b6cad209e39ef6a90fedc95a4468179725ac42/genera